from full_a import C
CARDS = {}
CARDS["andrew"] = [
 C("A seeker", "Andrew was a fisherman from Bethsaida — and a follower of John the Baptist, looking for the Messiah.",
 """Andrew and his older brother Peter grew up in Bethsaida, a fishing village where the Jordan River flows into the north end of the Sea of Galilee. The name Bethsaida means “house of fishing.” Later the brothers shared a house in Capernaum, a few miles west.

But Andrew wasn’t content just to fish. Around AD 27–28, news spread through Galilee about a prophet preaching in the wilderness near the Jordan River, about 70 miles south. John the Baptist dressed like the ancient prophet Elijah and told everyone — even religious leaders and soldiers — to turn from their sins and be baptized, because God’s promised King was about to appear.

Andrew made that long journey and became one of John’s disciples. That meant listening to John’s teaching, fasting and praying as he taught, and watching for the Messiah. It tells us something important about Andrew: he was spiritually hungry. He wasn’t satisfied with ordinary religion; he wanted God to act, and he was willing to leave home and work to be ready when He did.""", ("John",1)),
 C("The first to follow", "John pointed at Jesus and said, “Look, the Lamb of God!” Andrew followed Jesus and spent the whole day with Him.",
 """One day John the Baptist was standing by the Jordan with two of his disciples, Andrew and another man the Gospel doesn’t name (very likely John, son of Zebedee, who wrote that Gospel). Jesus walked by, and John the Baptist said, “Look, the Lamb of God!”

To a Jewish listener, “lamb of God” would bring to mind the Passover lamb whose blood saved Israel in Egypt, and the lambs sacrificed every day at the Temple for sin. The day before, John had said even more plainly: “Look, the Lamb of God, who takes away the sin of the world!”

Andrew didn’t wait. He and his friend left John and started walking after Jesus. Jesus turned around and asked them, “What do you want?” — or more literally, “What are you seeking?” They answered, “Rabbi, where are You staying?” It was a polite way of asking to spend time with Him. Jesus said, “Come, and you will see.”

They went with Him and stayed the rest of the day. John’s Gospel even remembers the time — “about the tenth hour,” around four in the afternoon. Whatever Jesus said in those hours, Andrew walked away convinced he had found the one he had been waiting for. He became, with his friend, one of the first two people in history to follow Jesus.""", ("John",1)),
 C("“We found the Messiah!”", "The first thing Andrew did was find his brother Simon: “We have found the Messiah.” Then he brought him to Jesus.",
 """John’s Gospel says that the very first thing Andrew did was find his own brother. He told him, “We have found the Messiah” — the anointed King promised by the prophets. Then he brought him to Jesus.

Jesus looked at Andrew’s brother and gave him a new name: Cephas, or Peter, meaning “rock.” From that moment Peter would become the leader of the apostles, the preacher at Pentecost and one of the most famous people in history — but it began with Andrew taking his brother by the arm.

Some time later, back in Galilee, Jesus officially called both brothers. He walked along the shore and saw them casting a net into the lake. “Come, follow Me,” Jesus said, “and I will send you out to fish for people.” At once they left their nets and followed Him. Andrew was also part of the small group Jesus sometimes took aside — Peter, Andrew, James and John — and was one of the four who asked Jesus privately on the Mount of Olives about the destruction of the Temple and the end of the age.

Andrew is never shown preaching to crowds. He is shown doing something just as important: making introductions.""", ("Mark",1)),
 C("The boy’s lunch", "When 5,000 men needed food, Andrew found a boy with five barley loaves and two small fish. Jesus fed everyone with it.",
 """Huge crowds had followed Jesus to a remote hillside near the Sea of Galilee, close to Andrew’s hometown of Bethsaida. It was nearly Passover. As evening came, there were about 5,000 men there, plus women and children, and nothing to eat.

Jesus asked Philip, “Where shall we buy bread for these people to eat?” Philip calculated that even eight months of wages wouldn’t buy enough for each person to have a bite.

Andrew had been doing something different: looking at what they actually had. He came to Jesus and said, “Here is a boy with five small barley loaves and two small fish.” Barley bread was the cheap bread of the poor, and the fish were probably small, dried or pickled. Then Andrew added his own honest doubt: “But how far will they go among so many?”

Jesus told everyone to sit down on the grass. He took the loaves, gave thanks, and handed them out, and did the same with the fish — as much as anyone wanted. When everyone was full, Jesus told the disciples to gather the leftovers so nothing would be wasted, and they filled twelve baskets. The people said, “Surely this is the Prophet who is to come into the world.”

Andrew didn’t understand how a child’s lunch could help. But he brought it to Jesus anyway — and that was enough.""", ("John",6)),
 C("Bringing the Greeks", "When some Greeks asked to see Jesus, Philip told Andrew, and together they brought them to Jesus.",
 """In Jesus’ final week, Jerusalem was crowded with pilgrims for Passover. Among them were some Greeks — Gentiles who worshiped the God of Israel. They came to Philip, who also had a Greek name and was from Bethsaida, and asked, “Sir, we would like to see Jesus.”

Philip wasn’t sure what to do. Jesus had mostly focused His ministry on Israel. So Philip went to Andrew — and together Andrew and Philip went and told Jesus.

Jesus’ response was striking. “The hour has come for the Son of Man to be glorified,” He said. “Unless a kernel of wheat falls to the ground and dies, it remains only a single seed. But if it dies, it produces many seeds.” Jesus was saying that His death was about to happen, and through it people from every nation would come to Him. The arrival of these Greeks was a sign of the worldwide harvest to come.

It is the third time John’s Gospel shows Andrew bringing someone to Jesus: his brother, a boy with a lunch, and now foreigners. Andrew seems to have understood early that Jesus was for everyone.""", ("John",12)),
 C("How it ended", "Andrew was in the upper room at Pentecost. The Bible doesn’t say what happened to him after that, but tradition has said he preached around the Black Sea and Greece and was crucified on an X-shaped cross in Patras.",
 """After Jesus rose from the dead and ascended to heaven, Andrew was with the other apostles in the upper room in Jerusalem, praying, when the Holy Spirit came at Pentecost. That is the last time the Bible names him.

The Bible doesn’t say what happened to Andrew after that, but tradition has said a great deal. The early church historian Eusebius, around AD 300, recorded that Andrew preached in Scythia — the region around the Black Sea, in parts of modern Ukraine, Romania and Russia. That’s why he is honored as a patron saint of Ukraine, Romania and Russia.

Tradition has also said that he preached in Greece and was crucified at Patras around AD 60 by the Roman governor. According to that account, Andrew asked to be crucified on an X-shaped cross rather than an upright one like Jesus’, and he was tied rather than nailed so that he would suffer longer — and he preached to the crowd from the cross for two days until he died.

The X-shaped cross, called the saltire or St. Andrew’s Cross, became his symbol. It forms the flag of Scotland, which adopted Andrew as its patron saint after legends that some of his relics were brought there. His feast day is November 30.""", ("Acts",1)),
]
CARDS["thomas"] = [
 C("The Twin", "Thomas was also called Didymus — Greek for “twin.” The Bible never says who his twin was.",
 """Thomas is a Greek form of the Aramaic word for “twin,” and John’s Gospel adds his Greek nickname, Didymus, which also means twin. The Bible never tells us who his twin was, where he was from, or what his work was. He was among the seven disciples fishing on the Sea of Galilee after the resurrection, so he may have been a fisherman from Galilee like several others.

Matthew, Mark and Luke only mention Thomas in their lists of the twelve. Almost everything we know about his personality comes from four short scenes in John’s Gospel. In every one, Thomas speaks his mind honestly — whether it’s courage, confusion, or doubt.""", ("John",11)),
 C("“Let us die with Him”", "When Jesus decided to return near Jerusalem, where people had tried to stone Him, Thomas said: “Let’s go too, so we may die with Him.”",
 """Not long before His death, Jesus had been teaching in Jerusalem during a winter festival, and the religious leaders picked up stones to kill Him for claiming to be one with God. Jesus withdrew across the Jordan River to safety.

Then a message came from Bethany, a village just two miles from Jerusalem: Jesus’ close friend Lazarus was sick. Jesus waited two days, then said, “Let us go back to Judea.” The disciples were alarmed: “Rabbi, a short while ago the Jews there tried to stone You, and yet You are going back?” Jesus told them plainly that Lazarus had died, and that He was going to wake him.

It was Thomas who spoke up to the rest of the group: “Let us also go, that we may die with Him.”

It sounds gloomy, maybe even fatalistic — Thomas didn’t expect it to end well. But it was also brave and loyal. He believed going back meant death, and he was willing to go anyway. “Doubting Thomas” was, first, Courageous Thomas.""", ("John",11)),
 C("“How can we know the way?”", "Jesus said, “You know the way to where I’m going.” Thomas said, “We don’t even know where You’re going.” Jesus: “I am the way.”",
 """At the Last Supper, Jesus began preparing His disciples for His death. He told them not to let their hearts be troubled: “My Father’s house has many rooms… I am going there to prepare a place for you… I will come back and take you to be with Me.” Then He added, “You know the way to the place where I am going.”

Thomas didn’t pretend to understand. “Lord, we don’t know where You are going, so how can we know the way?”

His honest confusion drew out one of the most famous sayings in the Bible. Jesus answered, “I am the way and the truth and the life. No one comes to the Father except through Me. If you really know Me, you will know My Father as well.”

Thomas wanted a map. Jesus gave him a person. If Thomas had nodded along like the others, that sentence might never have been spoken.""", ("John",14)),
 C("The doubt", "Thomas missed Jesus’ first appearance. He said he wouldn’t believe unless he touched the nail wounds.",
 """On the evening of the first Easter Sunday, the disciples were together behind locked doors, afraid of the religious leaders. Suddenly Jesus stood among them and said, “Peace be with you!” He showed them His hands and His side, and they were overjoyed.

But Thomas wasn’t there. The Bible doesn’t say why.

When the others told him, “We have seen the Lord!” Thomas refused to accept it secondhand. He had watched Jesus be crucified. He knew dead men don’t come back. He said, “Unless I see the nail marks in His hands and put my finger where the nails were, and put my hand into His side, I will not believe.”

It’s easy to criticize Thomas, but notice what he asked for: the same evidence the others had already received. And he stayed with the group for the next week, still in the room, still waiting.""", ("John",20)),
 C("“My Lord and my God!”", "A week later Jesus appeared again and said, “Touch them.” Thomas said: “My Lord and my God!”",
 """Eight days later, the disciples were in the house again, and this time Thomas was with them. The doors were locked, but Jesus came and stood among them: “Peace be with you!”

Then He turned straight to Thomas. Jesus quoted Thomas’s own words back to him — He had heard every one. “Put your finger here; see My hands. Reach out your hand and put it into My side. Stop doubting and believe.”

The Bible doesn’t say whether Thomas actually touched the wounds. It only records what he said: “My Lord and my God!” It is the clearest, most complete statement of who Jesus is anywhere in the Gospels — not just Teacher, not just Messiah, but Lord and God.

Jesus answered, “Because you have seen Me, you have believed; blessed are those who have not seen and yet have believed.” That blessing was for every future believer — including everyone reading this today. John then explains why he wrote his Gospel: so that you may believe that Jesus is the Messiah, the Son of God, and by believing have life in His name.""", ("John",20)),
 C("How it ended", "The Bible doesn’t say what happened to Thomas, but tradition has said he carried the gospel to India and was killed with a spear near Chennai around AD 72.",
 """Thomas appears once more in the Bible: fishing with Peter and others on the Sea of Galilee when the risen Jesus appeared on the shore. After that, he is listed among the apostles praying in Jerusalem before Pentecost.

The Bible doesn’t say what happened to Thomas after that, but tradition has said he traveled farther than any other apostle. Early writings say he preached in Parthia (modern Iran) and then sailed to India. The St. Thomas Christians of Kerala, in southwest India, have said for centuries that he landed on the Malabar Coast in AD 52 and founded seven churches. Their community is one of the oldest Christian communities in the world.

Tradition has said that Thomas was killed with a spear around AD 72 at a hill near Mylapore, in today’s city of Chennai. The San Thome Basilica in Chennai stands over his traditional tomb, and the hill is now called St. Thomas Mount.

The disciple remembered for doubting is said to have carried his faith to the far edge of the known world.""", ("John",21)),
]
CARDS["matthew"] = [
 C("A hated job", "Matthew, also called Levi, collected taxes in Capernaum for Herod and Rome. Tax collectors were seen as traitors and thieves.",
 """Capernaum sat on the international road known as the Way of the Sea, connecting Egypt with Damascus. Goods passing through, and fish sold from the lake, were taxed. Matthew — who Mark and Luke call Levi, son of Alphaeus — ran a tax booth there for Herod Antipas, the ruler of Galilee under Rome.

The system invited corruption. Tax collectors paid for the right to collect, and anything they charged above the official amount was their profit. Many got rich by overcharging their own neighbors, backed by soldiers. Jewish religious teachers grouped them with robbers and prostitutes; their money was considered dirty, and devout people wouldn’t eat with them.

So Matthew was probably wealthy, educated enough to keep records, and almost completely shunned by respectable society.""", ("Matthew",9)),
 C("Two words", "Jesus walked by his booth and said, “Follow Me.” Matthew got up, left everything, and followed.",
 """Jesus had been teaching and healing in Capernaum, often in Peter’s house a short walk away. Not long before, Jesus had healed a paralyzed man lowered through the roof of a house there, and had shocked the religious leaders by telling him, “Your sins are forgiven.” Matthew must have heard about it.

Then one day Jesus walked out along the lakeshore, past the tax booth, saw Matthew sitting there, and said, “Follow Me.”

Matthew got up and followed Him. Luke adds that he “left everything.” Fishermen like Peter could always return to their boats. A tax collector who abandoned his post couldn’t walk back in and get the contract again. Matthew’s decision was permanent, and costly.

Matthew’s own Gospel tells the story in just one verse, with no drama about himself — just the call and the response.""", ("Luke",5)),
 C("The party", "Matthew threw a big dinner for Jesus and invited his tax-collector friends. The religious leaders were scandalized.",
 """Matthew’s first act as a follower of Jesus was to throw a big banquet at his house in Jesus’ honor. And he invited the only friends he had: other tax collectors and people religious society considered “sinners.” A large crowd of them reclined at the table with Jesus and His disciples.

The Pharisees and their teachers were horrified. Sharing a meal meant accepting someone. They complained to Jesus’ disciples: “Why do you eat and drink with tax collectors and sinners?”

Jesus answered them directly: “It is not the healthy who need a doctor, but the sick. I have not come to call the righteous, but sinners to repentance.” Matthew’s Gospel adds that Jesus told them to go and learn what the prophet Hosea meant: “I desire mercy, not sacrifice.”

The dinner showed what Matthew understood about Jesus right from the start: He had come for people like him — and his friends.""", ("Luke",5)),
 C("His Gospel", "The first book of the New Testament has been credited to Matthew since the early church. It shows how Jesus fulfilled the Old Testament.",
 """The Gospel of Matthew doesn’t name its author, but the early church unanimously credited it to the apostle Matthew. Around AD 120, a bishop named Papias wrote that Matthew compiled the sayings of Jesus in the Hebrew language.

The Gospel is written especially for Jewish readers. It opens with Jesus’ family tree going back to Abraham and David, and it repeatedly says, “This took place to fulfill what the Lord had said through the prophet.” It contains more than 60 Old Testament quotations. It also gathers Jesus’ teaching into five big speeches, including the Sermon on the Mount, with the Beatitudes and the Lord’s Prayer.

Fittingly for a former tax collector, Matthew’s Gospel contains several stories about money and taxes, including one only he records: Jesus telling Peter to catch a fish with a coin in its mouth to pay the Temple tax. Matthew’s Gospel ends with the Great Commission: “Go and make disciples of all nations… And surely I am with you always, to the very end of the age.”""", ("Matthew",28)),
 C("How it ended", "Matthew was with the apostles at Pentecost. The Bible doesn’t say where he went after that, and traditions differ — Ethiopia, Persia or elsewhere — but most have said he died a martyr.",
 """Matthew is last named in the Bible among the apostles praying in Jerusalem after Jesus ascended, before the Holy Spirit came at Pentecost.

The Bible doesn’t say where he went after that. Eusebius wrote that Matthew first preached to the Jews in Judea before going to other nations. Different traditions have said he ministered in Ethiopia, in Persia, or among the Parthians, and most have said he died as a martyr, though the accounts don’t agree on how or where.

Matthew’s feast day is September 21 in the Western church. In art he is often shown with an angel or a winged man — the symbol of his Gospel, which begins with Jesus’ human family tree — and sometimes with a money bag, remembering where Jesus found him.""", ("Acts",1)),
]
CARDS["judas"] = [
 C("Chosen too", "Judas was one of the twelve. He was sent out to preach and heal like the others, and was trusted with the money.",
 """Judas son of Simon Iscariot was chosen by Jesus as one of the twelve apostles after Jesus prayed all night on a mountain. He traveled with Jesus for about three years. He saw the blind receive sight and the dead raised, heard the Sermon on the Mount, and watched Jesus feed thousands.

When Jesus sent the twelve out two by two to preach that the kingdom of heaven was near, heal the sick and drive out demons, Judas was among them. Nothing in the Gospels suggests the other disciples suspected him of anything. In fact, the group trusted him enough to make him the treasurer, keeping the common money bag used for their needs and for giving to the poor.""", ("Matthew",10)),
 C("A thief", "John says Judas kept the money bag and used to help himself to what was put in it.",
 """Six days before Passover, Jesus was at a dinner in Bethany with Lazarus, Martha and Mary. Mary took a pint of pure nard, an expensive perfume imported from India, poured it on Jesus’ feet, and wiped them with her hair. The whole house filled with the fragrance.

Judas objected loudly: “Why wasn’t this perfume sold and the money given to the poor? It was worth a year’s wages.” It sounded generous. But John, who knew him well, explains: “He did not say this because he cared about the poor but because he was a thief; as keeper of the money bag, he used to help himself to what was put into it.”

Jesus defended Mary: “Leave her alone. It was intended that she should save this perfume for the day of My burial.” Matthew and Mark place Judas’s visit to the chief priests right after this dinner.""", ("John",12)),
 C("The deal", "Judas went to the chief priests: “What will you give me to hand Him over?” They counted out 30 pieces of silver.",
 """The chief priests and elders had already gathered at the palace of Caiaphas, the high priest, and agreed to arrest Jesus secretly and kill Him — but not during the festival, because they feared a riot among the crowds who loved Him.

Judas solved their problem. He went to them and asked, “What are you willing to give me if I deliver Him over to you?” They counted out 30 pieces of silver. Under the law of Moses, that was the payment for a slave who was killed, and the prophet Zechariah had described being valued at that same insulting price. Luke and John both say that Satan entered Judas.

From then on, Judas watched for an opportunity to hand Jesus over when no crowd was present.""", ("Matthew",26)),
 C("The kiss", "In Gethsemane at night, Judas led soldiers to Jesus and identified Him with a kiss. Jesus said, “Judas, are you betraying Me with a kiss?”",
 """At the Last Supper, Jesus washed all the disciples’ feet, including Judas’s. Then He said, deeply troubled, “One of you is going to betray Me.” The disciples were stunned and asked, “Surely you don’t mean me, Lord?” Jesus said it was the one to whom He would give a piece of bread dipped in the dish, and He handed it to Judas. In that culture, offering someone a choice morsel was a gesture of honor and friendship. Jesus told him, “What you are about to do, do quickly.” Judas went out. John adds: “And it was night.”

Hours later, Judas led a large crowd with swords, clubs, torches and lanterns into the garden of Gethsemane — a place he knew because Jesus often met there with His disciples. He had given them a signal: “The one I kiss is the man; arrest Him.” A kiss was the normal greeting of a disciple to his teacher. Judas walked up, said, “Greetings, Rabbi!” and kissed Him.

Jesus answered, “Do what you came for, friend,” and in Luke, “Judas, are you betraying the Son of Man with a kiss?” Then the soldiers seized Him.""", ("Luke",22)),
 C("How it ended", "When Jesus was condemned, Judas regretted it, threw the silver into the Temple, and hanged himself.",
 """Early Friday morning, the council decided to hand Jesus over to Pilate to be executed. When Judas saw that Jesus was condemned, Matthew says he was seized with remorse. He brought the 30 pieces of silver back to the chief priests and elders and said, “I have sinned, for I have betrayed innocent blood.”

They answered coldly, “What is that to us? That’s your responsibility.” Judas threw the money into the Temple and went away and hanged himself.

The priests picked up the coins but said it was against the law to put blood money into the Temple treasury. So they used it to buy a potter’s field as a burial place for foreigners, which became known as the Field of Blood. Acts adds more details about how his body was found in that field.

Peter later said that Judas “was one of our number and shared in our ministry,” and the apostles chose Matthias to take his place.""", ("Matthew",27)),
]
CARDS["james-zebedee"] = [
 C("Leaving the boat", "James and John were mending nets with their father Zebedee when Jesus called them. They left the boat — and their father — and followed.",
 """James and his younger brother John worked in their father Zebedee’s fishing business on the Sea of Galilee, in partnership with Peter and Andrew. Zebedee employed hired men, so the business was successful. Their mother, probably named Salome, would later follow Jesus too.

James was there the morning of the miraculous catch, when Peter’s nets nearly broke and the partners’ boats almost sank. Soon after, Jesus walked along the shore and saw James and John in a boat with their father, preparing their nets. He called them, and immediately they left the boat and their father and followed Him.

Mark adds the detail that they left Zebedee “in the boat with the hired men” — the business would go on, but the sons were gone. James is always named before John in the lists, likely because he was older.""", ("Mark",1)),
 C("Sons of Thunder", "Jesus nicknamed James and John “Boanerges” — Sons of Thunder. It fit.",
 """When Jesus named His twelve apostles, He gave James and John a nickname: Boanerges, which Mark translates as “Sons of Thunder.” The Gospels show why.

As Jesus set out on His final journey to Jerusalem, He sent messengers ahead into a Samaritan village to get things ready. Jews and Samaritans had a long, bitter hostility, and the village refused to welcome Him because He was heading to Jerusalem. James and John were furious. They asked, “Lord, do You want us to call fire down from heaven to destroy them?” — just as the prophet Elijah once did.

Jesus turned and rebuked them, and they went on to another village. James and John were fiercely loyal and passionate, but they had to learn that Jesus came to save people, not destroy them.""", ("Luke",9)),
 C("The inner circle", "James was one of only three disciples Jesus took into His most private moments.",
 """Out of the twelve, Jesus chose three — Peter, James and John — to be with Him at three of the most important moments of His ministry.

First, when a synagogue leader named Jairus begged Jesus to heal his dying 12-year-old daughter, and she died before they arrived, Jesus let no one follow Him into the house except Peter, James and John and the girl’s parents. They watched Him take her hand and say, “Talitha koum” — “Little girl, get up” — and she stood up.

Second, Jesus took the three up a high mountain, where they saw Him transfigured, shining like the sun, talking with Moses and Elijah, and heard God’s voice from the cloud.

Third, on the night He was betrayed, Jesus took the same three deeper into the garden of Gethsemane and asked them to stay awake and pray while He agonized over the cross. James saw Jesus’ greatest glory and His deepest suffering.""", ("Mark",5)),
 C("Asking for the best seats", "James and John asked to sit at Jesus’ right and left in His kingdom. Jesus asked: “Can you drink the cup I drink?”",
 """On the road to Jerusalem, right after Jesus told the twelve for the third time that He would be mocked, flogged and killed and rise again, James and John came to Him with a request. (Matthew says their mother came with them and knelt to ask.) “Teacher, we want You to do for us whatever we ask… Let one of us sit at Your right and the other at Your left in Your glory.”

They still imagined Jesus would set up an earthly throne, and they wanted the top two positions. Jesus said, “You don’t know what you are asking. Can you drink the cup I drink or be baptized with the baptism I am baptized with?” They answered confidently, “We can.” Jesus told them, “You will drink the cup I drink” — they would share in His suffering — but the places of honor were not His to grant.

When the other ten heard about it, they were angry with the brothers. So Jesus called them all together and said: among the rulers of this world, the powerful lord it over others. “Not so with you. Instead, whoever wants to become great among you must be your servant… For even the Son of Man did not come to be served, but to serve, and to give His life as a ransom for many.”""", ("Mark",10)),
 C("The first apostle to die", "About AD 44, King Herod Agrippa had James killed with the sword — the first of the twelve to be martyred.",
 """After Pentecost, James was one of the leaders of the church in Jerusalem. About eleven years after the resurrection, King Herod Agrippa I — grandson of Herod the Great — began arresting believers to please the religious leaders. Acts records it in a single verse: Herod “had James, the brother of John, put to death with the sword.”

James became the first of the twelve apostles to die for his faith — the only apostle whose death is recorded in the Bible. Years before, he had told Jesus he could drink His cup, and Jesus had told him he would. He did.

Eusebius preserves a story from Clement of Alexandria that the man who brought James to trial was so moved by his testimony that he declared himself a Christian too, and the two were executed together.

The Bible doesn’t say where James was buried, but Spanish tradition has said his remains were carried to Spain and rest at Santiago de Compostela — “St. James of the field of the star” — the destination of the Camino de Santiago pilgrimage, walked by hundreds of thousands of people every year.""", ("Acts",12)),
]
